WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:01.650
TOM SCOTT: I got an email that said:

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:05.549
"Do you want to come and see our satellite
clean room"?

00:00:05.549 --> 00:00:08.550
Yes. Yes I do.

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:11.360
(How do you get into this thing?)

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:13.230
WOUTER WEGGELAAR: This is the clean room area.

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:17.050
We have a working environment here for the
people to work clean.

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:18.640
Basically this is a box in a box.

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:22.019
And on the roof we have some filters

00:00:22.019 --> 00:00:24.179
and also in the walls we have ventilation

00:00:24.179 --> 00:00:26.400
so the air is always going downwards

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:29.259
and this is also a laminar flow cleanroom

00:00:29.259 --> 00:00:31.310
so the air is also going straight down

00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:32.759
instead of in vortexes.

00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:36.390
TS: This is Innovative Space Logistics in
the Netherlands.

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:38.980
Originally a spinoff from TU Delft's engineering
department,

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:41.960
they now launch Cubesats, like this one --

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:43.949
okay, it's a model, that's why I'm allowed
to touch it --

00:00:43.949 --> 00:00:47.410
but they launch satellites like this into
actual space.

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:49.379
I mean, the rockets go up from all over the
world,

00:00:49.379 --> 00:00:53.089
but some of the satellites that are on board?
Put together right here.

00:00:53.089 --> 00:00:57.089
WW: Well, the cage behind me is a customs
area

00:00:57.089 --> 00:01:00.879
where we have the satellites enter the clean
room

00:01:00.879 --> 00:01:02.739
but not the Netherlands

00:01:02.739 --> 00:01:04.350
and that's for export reasons.

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:07.000
Technically we're exporting these satellites
to a country

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:08.390
which is the country of launch

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:10.670
but they are exporting to space

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:13.409
and space doesn't have a box on the forms!

00:01:13.409 --> 00:01:15.890
TS: There are international standards for
cleanrooms.

00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:19.070
They go from ISO 9, which is just normal air,

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:21.310
to ISO 1. Now this room is only ISO 7,

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:25.049
which doesn't sound very impressive until
you realise it's a log scale:

00:01:25.049 --> 00:01:29.180
each level of that standard requires a tenth
of the dust and particulates.

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:34.110
So this room guarantees less than 1% of the
dust outside it.

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:36.200
WW: So the main contamination source --

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:38.140
and that's also why I'm wearing all of this
--

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:42.829
is particles that are released from our own
bodies.

00:01:42.829 --> 00:01:48.420
Dead skin cells, hairs. If you smoke, even
air you exhale.

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:51.490
Beard, in my case, because I have a beard
mask on.

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:54.619
Cardboard, paper, velcro --

00:01:54.619 --> 00:01:57.859
basically stuff that can hold particles and
release it when you don't want to.

00:01:57.859 --> 00:02:00.840
TS: And it's not just the dust particles that
you can see.

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:02.920
Those are actually pretty easy.

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:05.039
Cleanrooms also have to remove microparticles,

00:02:05.039 --> 00:02:07.420
things smaller than a bacterium.

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:09.050
You don't have to get them all,

00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:11.050
but you do have to get most of them.

00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:15.349
WW: This is a small communications satellite
called Nayif-1.

00:02:15.349 --> 00:02:18.840
And this satellite will be a communication
experiment

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:22.590
and also will be used in classrooms for teaching
about space.

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:25.590
Schools can pick up the signals from this
satellite

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:27.450
decode them in the classroom

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:30.140
and teach about space, teach about radio.

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:34.220
TS: In here, the worst-case scenario is contamination
on a camera lens,

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:40.090
or a vacuum gyroscope that fails while it's
doing 10,000 RPM... in space.

00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:42.489
This down here is an actual satellite,

00:02:42.489 --> 00:02:43.940
it's headed for space later this year,

00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:45.590
and I've got to stand back from it,

00:02:45.590 --> 00:02:47.690
because if I lean over, dust from me,

00:02:47.690 --> 00:02:49.879
or spittle from my mouth, could fall down.

00:02:49.879 --> 00:02:51.550
Won't break anything right now,

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:53.510
but in zero-gravity, in vacuum,

00:02:53.510 --> 00:02:56.489
with direct solar radiation shining down on
it?

00:02:56.489 --> 00:02:59.590
You can't exactly call a breakdown service
to fix your satellite.

00:02:59.590 --> 00:03:03.209
So for safety's sake, it's kept very, very
clean.

00:03:03.209 --> 00:03:07.510
But those ISO 1 cleanrooms? They're for very
different missions.

00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:10.989
WW: ISO 1 cleanrooms is very clean,

00:03:10.989 --> 00:03:14.250
and basically for Mars missions, or missions
to the moon,

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:18.690
where we do not want to contaminate the surface
of a planet or a body that we go to.

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:20.900
TS: Are you sending anything to Mars any time
soon?

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:22.909
WW: Not at the moment, it's not planned

00:03:22.909 --> 00:03:26.220
but maybe in the future we will send something
to Mars! You never know.

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:29.380
